s time it had been replaced by more modern aircraft such as the Bristol Blenheim and Vickers Wellesley bombers. A total of 209 Vildebeests were manufactured, including quite a few variants.","LocalizedString":"The Vickers Vildebeest was a large two to three seated single engined biplane that was used by the RAF as a light bomber, torpedo bomber and in army cooperation roles. The Vildebeest flew its first flight in 1928 and was used in service up until 1942. At the outbreak of WWII the aircraft was considered obsolete, but 101 still remained in service although they were all scheduled to be replaced by aircraft such as the Bristol Beaufort. However, two squadrons based in Singapore were still waiting for their Beauforts when Japan invaded Malaya in 1941 and they had no choice but to send the obsolete biplanes against the attackers. During these attacks the Vildebeests had no fighter escort and suffered heavy losses from Japanese fighters. On January 26, 1942,  two squadrons with 12 Vildebeests were sent against the Japanese landing forces at Endau, which was only 250 miles from Singapore, and despite being escorted by Brewster Buffalo and Hawker Hurricane fighters, 5 Vildebeests were lost. The attack was repeated that same day, this time with 8 Vildebeests and 3 Fairey Albacores, but this resulted in 6 more Vildebeests being lost plus 3 Albacores. The surviving Vildebeests were withdrawn to Java where they continued operating against the Japanese forces to some success, but by March 1942 the last Vildebeests in RAF service was retired. By this time it had been replaced by more modern aircraft such as the Bristol Blenheim and Vickers Wellesley bombers. A total of 209 Vildebeests were manufactured, including quite a few variants."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BrothersInArms","isReserved":true,"EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardDealDamageAddDamage","ERegisteredCardFunc